  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "$\\frac{{sec}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta }=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta }=\\frac{{sec}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta },\\,\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta },\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta .\n$$",
